{"parentUuid":"a9a2dd00-4c14-452e-a7c7-cbf4edc13169","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"output_style_instructions","style":{"name":"ELI5","prompt":"It's been a long day and my brain is fried, talk to me like I'm 5.\nOnly report to me in ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English. That means\napproved plain words, active voice, and one instruction or idea per sentence.\nSmall words, short sentences, short paragraphs. If you have to use\na big word, explain it right after. Only return what's actually necessary.\nJust tell me what you did, did it work, what do I do now.\nIf I have to decide something: 2 options max, the context I need to pick fast,\nand which one you'd go with.\nKeep paths and commands exact. I have no brain cells left for the rest."}},"type":"attachment","uuid":"145fe569-eba1-4cf8-be6c-3606e2f82faf","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:43:26.602Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n# Output Style: ELI5\nIt's been a long day and my brain is fried, talk to me like I'm 5.\nOnly report to me in ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English. That means\napproved plain words, active voice, and one instruction or idea per sentence.\nSmall words, short sentences, short paragraphs. If you have to use\na big word, explain it right after. Only return what's actually necessary.\nJust tell me what you did, did it work, what do I do now.\nIf I have to decide something: 2 options max, the context I need to pick fast,\nand which one you'd go with.\nKeep paths and commands exact. I have no brain cells left for the rest.\n</system-reminder>"}],"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"35d4f785-606b-402b-829e-4e9c3081934b","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"145fe569-eba1-4cf8-be6c-3606e2f82faf","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_delta","addedNames":["ArtifactComments","ArtifactData","CronCreate","CronDelete","CronList","DesignSync","EndConversation","EnterPlanMode","ExitPlanMode","ExitWorktree","FetchInboxMessage","ListMcpResourcesTool","Monitor","NotebookEdit","PushNotification","ReadMcpResourceDirTool","ReadMcpResourceTool","RemoteTrigger","SendMessage","SendUserFile","TaskCreate","TaskGet","TaskList","TaskOutput","TaskStop","TaskUpdate","WebFetch","WebSearch","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_doctor","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_index","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_insight","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_purge","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_stats","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_upgrade","mcp__plugin_exa_exa__authenticate","mcp__plugin_exa_exa__complete_authentication"],"addedLines":["ArtifactComments","ArtifactData","CronCreate","CronDelete","CronList","DesignSync","EndConversation","EnterPlanMode","ExitPlanMode","ExitWorktree","FetchInboxMessage","ListMcpResourcesTool","Monitor","NotebookEdit","PushNotification","ReadMcpResourceDirTool","ReadMcpResourceTool","RemoteTrigger","SendMessage","SendUserFile","TaskCreate","TaskGet","TaskList","TaskOutput","TaskStop","TaskUpdate","WebFetch","WebSearch","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_doctor","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_index","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_insight","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_purge","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_stats","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_upgrade","mcp__plugin_exa_exa__authenticate","mcp__plugin_exa_exa__complete_authentication"],"removedNames":[],"wireHiddenNames":[],"readdedNames":[],"pendingMcpServers":["claude-in-chrome","claude.ai Claude Docs","claude.ai Excalidraw","claude.ai Fireflies","claude.ai Gmail","claude.ai Google Calendar","claude.ai Google Drive","claude.ai HyperFrames by HeyGen","plugin:browser-use:browser-use","plugin:desktop-commander:desktop-commander","zotero"],"failedMcpServers":[]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6887fe9c-beaa-4253-8e02-2a7ace20b980","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:43:26.602Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nThe following deferred tools are now available via ToolSearch. Their schemas are NOT loaded — calling them directly will fail with InputValidationError. Use ToolSearch with query \"select:<name>[,<name>...]\" to load tool schemas before calling them:\nArtifactComments\nArtifactData\nCronCreate\nCronDelete\nCronList\nDesignSync\nEndConversation\nEnterPlanMode\nExitPlanMode\nExitWorktree\nFetchInboxMessage\nListMcpResourcesTool\nMonitor\nNotebookEdit\nPushNotification\nReadMcpResourceDirTool\nReadMcpResourceTool\nRemoteTrigger\nSendMessage\nSendUserFile\nTaskCreate\nTaskGet\nTaskList\nTaskOutput\nTaskStop\nTaskUpdate\nWebFetch\nWebSearch\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_doctor\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_index\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_insight\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_purge\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_stats\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_upgrade\nmcp__plugin_exa_exa__authenticate\nmcp__plugin_exa_exa__complete_authentication\n\nThe following MCP servers are still connecting — their tools (typically named mcp__<server>__*) are not yet available but will appear shortly:\nclaude-in-chrome\nclaude.ai Claude Docs\nclaude.ai Excalidraw\nclaude.ai Fireflies\nclaude.ai Gmail\nclaude.ai Google Calendar\nclaude.ai Google Drive\nclaude.ai HyperFrames by HeyGen\nplugin:browser-use:browser-use\nplugin:desktop-commander:desktop-commander\nzotero\n\nIf the user's request might be served by one of these servers (even if they didn't name it explicitly), call ToolSearch with a relevant keyword — ToolSearch will wait for connecting servers and search their tools once available. Do not report a capability as unavailable without first searching.\n</system-reminder>"}],"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"35d4f785-606b-402b-829e-4e9c3081934b","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"6887fe9c-beaa-4253-8e02-2a7ace20b980","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"agent_listing_delta","addedTypes":["claude","claude-code-guide","codex:codex-rescue","Explore","general-purpose","Plan","statusline-setup"],"addedLines":["- claude: Catch-all for any task that doesn't fit a more specific agent. FleetView's default when no agent name is typed. (Tools: *)","- claude-code-guide: Use this agent when the user asks questions (\"Can Claude...\", \"Does Claude...\", \"How do I...\") about: (1) Claude Code (the CLI tool) - features, hooks, slash commands, MCP servers, settings, IDE integrations, keyboard shortcuts; (2) Claude Agent SDK - building custom agents; (3) Claude API (formerly Anthropic API) - Messages API for directly passing messages to Claude, Tool Runner (`client.beta.messages.tool_runner`) for running an agentic loop over your own tools, manual tool-use loops, Managed Agents for server-hosted agents with a managed sandbox, prompt caching, and general Anthropic SDK usage; (4) Claude Tag (Claude in Slack) - what it is, setting it up for a Slack workspace, `/install-slack-app`; (5) `claude plugin eval` (writing and running plugin eval suites, its JSON/report, sandbox, CI) and the `/skill-doctor` report. **IMPORTANT:** Before spawning a new agent, check if there is already a running or recently completed claude-code-guide agent that you can continue via SendMessage. (Tools: Bash, Read, WebFetch, WebSearch)","- codex:codex-rescue: Proactively use when Claude Code is stuck, wants a second implementation or diagnosis pass, needs a deeper root-cause investigation, or should hand a substantial coding task to Codex through the shared runtime (Tools: Bash)","- Explore: Read-only search agent for broad fan-out searches — when answering means sweeping many files, directories, or naming conventions and you only need the conclusion, not the file dumps. It reads excerpts rather than whole files, so it locates code; it doesn't review or audit it. Specify search breadth: \"medium\" for moderate exploration, \"very thorough\" for multiple locations and naming conventions. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ArtifactComments, ArtifactData, ArtifactCheck, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)","- general-purpose: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. When you are searching for a keyword or file and are not confident that you will find the right match in the first few tries use this agent to perform the search for you. (Tools: *)","- Plan: Software architect agent for designing implementation plans. Use this when you need to plan the implementation strategy for a task. Returns step-by-step plans, identifies critical files, and considers architectural trade-offs. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ArtifactComments, ArtifactData, ArtifactCheck, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)","- statusline-setup: Use this agent to configure the user's Claude Code status line setting. (Tools: Read, Edit)"],"removedTypes":[],"isInitial":true,"showConcurrencyNote":true},"type":"attachment","uuid":"ee9c735d-9326-4c16-a91d-7689ac4bcc9b","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:43:26.602Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nAvailable agent types for the Agent tool:\n- claude: Catch-all for any task that doesn't fit a more specific agent. FleetView's default when no agent name is typed. (Tools: *)\n- claude-code-guide: Use this agent when the user asks questions (\"Can Claude...\", \"Does Claude...\", \"How do I...\") about: (1) Claude Code (the CLI tool) - features, hooks, slash commands, MCP servers, settings, IDE integrations, keyboard shortcuts; (2) Claude Agent SDK - building custom agents; (3) Claude API (formerly Anthropic API) - Messages API for directly passing messages to Claude, Tool Runner (`client.beta.messages.tool_runner`) for running an agentic loop over your own tools, manual tool-use loops, Managed Agents for server-hosted agents with a managed sandbox, prompt caching, and general Anthropic SDK usage; (4) Claude Tag (Claude in Slack) - what it is, setting it up for a Slack workspace, `/install-slack-app`; (5) `claude plugin eval` (writing and running plugin eval suites, its JSON/report, sandbox, CI) and the `/skill-doctor` report. **IMPORTANT:** Before spawning a new agent, check if there is already a running or recently completed claude-code-guide agent that you can continue via SendMessage. (Tools: Bash, Read, WebFetch, WebSearch)\n- codex:codex-rescue: Proactively use when Claude Code is stuck, wants a second implementation or diagnosis pass, needs a deeper root-cause investigation, or should hand a substantial coding task to Codex through the shared runtime (Tools: Bash)\n- Explore: Read-only search agent for broad fan-out searches — when answering means sweeping many files, directories, or naming conventions and you only need the conclusion, not the file dumps. It reads excerpts rather than whole files, so it locates code; it doesn't review or audit it. Specify search breadth: \"medium\" for moderate exploration, \"very thorough\" for multiple locations and naming conventions. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ArtifactComments, ArtifactData, ArtifactCheck, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)\n- general-purpose: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. When you are searching for a keyword or file and are not confident that you will find the right match in the first few tries use this agent to perform the search for you. (Tools: *)\n- Plan: Software architect agent for designing implementation plans. Use this when you need to plan the implementation strategy for a task. Returns step-by-step plans, identifies critical files, and considers architectural trade-offs. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ArtifactComments, ArtifactData, ArtifactCheck, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)\n- statusline-setup: Use this agent to configure the user's Claude Code status line setting. (Tools: Read, Edit)\n\nWhen you launch multiple agents for independent work, send them in a single message with multiple tool uses so they run concurrently.\n</system-reminder>"}],"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"35d4f785-606b-402b-829e-4e9c3081934b","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"ee9c735d-9326-4c16-a91d-7689ac4bcc9b","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"skill_listing","content":"- anti-sleep\n- ArXiv\n- backup\n- brain-audit\n- capture-insight\n- claude-stack-doctor: Health-check Eric's automation/memory stack in one pass and verify the Mac-to-Windows handoff works: launchd agents, Python venvs, memsearch, the memory-compiler flush pipeline, backup freshness, and context budget. Use when the user says \"/claude-stack-doctor\", \"stack doctor\", \"is my stack healthy\", \"did my scheduled jobs run\", \"check the memory pipeline\", \"why didn't the backup run\", or when closeout/morning sessions should verify automation health. Do not use to run the backup itself (use backup) or to audit context-engineering practice (use doctor-plus).\n- claudex-loop\n- claudex-route\n- cli-hub-meta-skill\n- closeout\n- code-review\n- codex-build\n- codex-review: Have Codex independently review an existing implementation plan while Claude coordinates a bounded revision loop. For automatic reviewer selection from either host, use claudex-loop with mode=review.\n- CreateSkill: Invoke BEFORE any skill work — creating, modifying, adding a workflow/tool to, renaming, validating, or canonicalizing any skill. Required by skills/CLAUDE.md; the single home for skill authoring in this workspace. Owns the skill lifecycle: scaffold (public TitleCase vs private _ALLCAPS, flat 2-level, Gotchas + Examples, BPE check), validate, canonicalize, test, improve, optimize descriptions. USE WHEN create skill, new skill, make a skill, build a skill, set up a skill, private skill, make a X skill, add a workflow, add a tool, edit/change/update/rename a skill, skill frontmatter, validate skill, check skill, canonicalize, scaffold skill, test skill, improve skill, optimize description, skill not triggering, overtriggering. NOT FOR TypeScript CLI generation (use CreateCLI).\n- design-taste-frontend\n- dev-sync\n- doctor-plus\n- fireflies-intake: One-command Fireflies pipeline — sync new meeting transcripts into the SecondBrain vault, rename generically-titled meetings, then report exactly what was ingested. Use when the user says \"/fireflies-intake\", \"ingest my recent fireflies meetings\", \"sync meetings that aren't in the second brain yet\", \"pull in the new meetings\", \"meeting ingestion\", or asks whether all recent meetings have been ingested. Do not use to search what was said in transcripts (use pp-fireflies).\n- five-slides-generator\n- forecast-capture\n- graphify: Use for any question about a codebase, its architecture, file relationships, or project content — especially when graphify-out/ exists, where the question should be treated as a graphify query first. Turns any input (code, docs, papers, images, videos) into a persistent knowledge graph with god nodes, community detection, and query/path/explain tools. Do not use for a quick lookup of one known file or symbol, or for sport-science literature questions (use sports-science-research-grounding).\n- harvest\n- html-to-pdf: Render any local HTML report, deck, or dashboard to a print-ready PDF via headless Chrome — replaces the manual Ctrl+P → Save as PDF ritual. Use when the user says \"export this to PDF\", \"print version\", \"PDF for the coaches\", \"make this printable\", or a coach-facing HTML deliverable needs a shareable/printable copy. Do not use to build the report or deck itself (use status-report or five-slides-generator).\n- impeccable: Use when the user wants to design, redesign, shape, critique, audit, polish, clarify, distill, harden, optimize, adapt, animate, colorize, extract, or otherwise improve a frontend interface. Covers websites, landing pages, dashboards, product UI, app shells, components, forms, settings, onboarding, and empty states. Handles UX review, visual hierarchy, information architecture, cognitive load, accessibility, performance, responsive behavior, theming, anti-patterns, typography, fonts, spacing, layout, alignment, color, motion, micro-interactions, UX copy, error states, edge cases, i18n, and reusable design systems or tokens. Also use for bland designs that need to become bolder or more delightful, loud designs that should become quieter, live browser iteration on UI elements, or ambitious visual effects that should feel technically extraordinary. Not for backend-only or non-UI tasks.\n- improve-system\n- ingest-book\n- ingest-resource\n- interview-me\n- ISA\n- library-advisor\n- library-review\n- meeting-prep: Pre-meeting readiness brief for a project — what changed, what's open, every deliverable path. Use when the user says \"/meeting-prep <project>\", \"I have a meeting with <person> — are we in a good spot on <project>?\", \"verify we're up to speed before the meeting\", \"get me ready for this afternoon's meeting\", or names an upcoming coach/staff meeting. Do not use for the biweekly supervisor report (use status-report) or to sync meeting transcripts (use meeting-sync).\n- meeting-sync\n- memory-write: Writes, updates, removes, and recalls facts in the two-tier memory system (~/.claude/global-memory/ and ~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/). Use for write requests such as \"remember this\", \"note that\", \"update memory\", \"save this\", and \"forget about\", and for recall requests such as \"did we already…?\", \"what did we decide about X?\", and \"do you remember…?\". Picks the tier, dedups, and enforces caps; recall checks the injected snapshot, then memsearch. Do not use for a story or lesson worth a vault note (use capture-insight) or an article to keep (use ingest-resource).\n- name-meetings: Detects Fireflies meetings with generic titles (empty, \"Zoom Meeting\", \"Recording\", date-only, etc.), proposes structured names based on AI summary + participants, and applies approved renames via the Fireflies API. Use when user says \"/name-meetings\", \"name my meetings\", \"rename untitled meetings\", \"fix meeting titles\", \"my meetings have no names\", or when invoked by meeting-sync. Do not use to sync transcripts or audit vault filenames (use meeting-sync).\n- new-project\n- next-decision\n- pbir-report-builder\n- persistent-localhost\n- pp-catapult-openfield-connect\n- pp-zotero-research-library\n- project: Manage the portfolio project registry (ideas, updates, ranking, promotion, dev stages) and render/publish the dashboard. Use when the user says \"/project\", \"new idea:\", \"log progress on\", \"project update\", \"add to my project inbox\", \"show my projects\", \"re-rank my projects\", \"set the stage on\", \"mark <project> as spec'd/planned/shipped\". Do not use for the DEV drift dashboard (use project-triage) or to scaffold a new repo (use new-project).\n- project-triage\n- Science\n- scroll-craft\n- session-miner\n- sports-science-research-grounding: Use when the user asks a substantive question about sport science, return to play or sport, rehabilitation, strength and conditioning, workload, recovery, testing, athlete health, injury, performance, or wants a research-backed answer, and for Zotero full-library batch processing. Searches the user's Zotero library first, clearly labels external peer-reviewed fallback sources, and applies tier-aware model routing during bounded batch work. Do not use for sports scores or schedules (use pp-espn) or for a simple Zotero search or sync (use pp-zotero-research-library).\n- startup\n- status-report: Build the every-two-weeks Applied Performance status report for Kenny Boyd and Chris Ruf — paste-ready email, one-page PDF, internal record. Use when the user says \"/status-report\", \"build my status report\", \"time for the Chris report\", \"biweekly update\", \"status update for Chris and Kenny\", \"what do I send Chris this week\", or when a scheduled report date arrives. Do not use for a single meeting brief (use meeting-prep) or the end-of-day supervisor log interview (use interview-me).\n- weekly-review\n- codex:rescue: Delegate investigation, an explicit fix request, or follow-up rescue work to the Codex rescue subagent\n- codex:setup: Check whether the local Codex CLI is ready and optionally toggle the stop-time review gate\n- firecrawl:skill-gen: Generate a complete Agent Skill from a documentation URL using Firecrawl\n- superpowers:brainstorming: You MUST use this before any creative work - creating features, building components, adding functionality, or modifying behavior. Explores user intent, requirements and design before implementation.\n- superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents: Use when facing 2+ independent tasks that can be worked on without shared state or sequential dependencies\n- superpowers:executing-plans: Use when you have a written implementation plan to execute in a separate session with review checkpoints\n- superpowers:finishing-a-development-branch: Use when implementation is complete, all tests pass, and you need to decide how to integrate the work\n- superpowers:receiving-code-review: Use when receiving code review feedback, before implementing suggestions, especially if feedback seems unclear or technically questionable - requires technical rigor and verification, not performative agreement or blind implementation\n- superpowers:requesting-code-review: Use when completing tasks, implementing major features, or before merging to verify work meets requirements\n- superpowers:subagent-driven-development: Use when executing implementation plans with independent tasks in the current session\n- superpowers:systematic-debugging: Use when encountering any bug, test failure, or unexpected behavior, before proposing fixes\n- superpowers:test-driven-development: Use when implementing any feature or bugfix, before writing implementation code\n- superpowers:using-git-worktrees: Use when starting feature work that needs isolation from current workspace or before executing implementation plans - ensures an isolated workspace exists via native tools or git worktree fallback\n- superpowers:using-superpowers: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n- superpowers:verification-before-completion: Use when about to claim work is complete, fixed, or passing, before committing or creating PRs - requires running verification commands and confirming output before making any success claims; evidence before assertions always\n- superpowers:writing-plans: Use when you have a spec or requirements for a multi-step task, before touching code\n- superpowers:writing-skills: Use when creating new skills, editing existing skills, or verifying skills work before deployment\n- last30days:last30days: Research what people actually say about any topic in the last 30 days. Pulls posts and engagement from Reddit, X, YouTube, TikTok, Hacker News, Polymarket, GitHub, and the web. Includes a doctor health check to diagnose broken or missing sources.\n- context-mode:context-mode: Use context-mode tools (ctx_execute, ctx_execute_file) instead of Bash/cat when processing\nlarge outputs. Triggers: \"analyze logs\", \"summarize output\", \"process data\",\n\"parse JSON\", \"filter results\", \"extract errors\", \"check build output\",\n\"analyze dependencies\", \"process API response\", \"large file analysis\",\n\"page snapshot\", \"browser snapshot\", \"DOM structure\", \"inspect page\",\n\"accessibility tree\", \"Playwright snapshot\",\n\"run tests\", \"test output\", \"coverage report\", \"git log\", \"recent commits\",\n\"diff between branches\", \"list containers\", \"pod status\", \"disk usage\",\n\"fetch docs\", \"API reference\", \"index documentation\",\n\"call API\", \"check response\", \"query results\",\n\"find TODOs\", \"count lines\", \"codebase statistics\", \"security audit\",\n\"outdated packages\", \"dependency tree\", \"cloud resources\", \"CI/CD output\".\nAlso triggers on ANY MCP tool output that may exceed 20 lines.\nSubagent routing is handled automatically via PreToolUse hook.\n- context-mode:ctx-doctor: Run context-mode diagnostics. Checks runtimes, hooks, FTS5,\nplugin registration, npm and marketplace versions.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-doctor\n- context-mode:ctx-index: Index a local file or directory into context-mode's persistent FTS5 knowledge base\nso future ctx_search calls can retrieve focused snippets without rereading raw files.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-index\n- context-mode:ctx-insight: Open the context-mode Insight dashboard in your default browser.\nInsight is the hosted analytics layer for AI-assisted engineering teams —\nper-engineer productive rate, retry waste, blocker detection, role-narrowed views.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-insight\n- context-mode:ctx-purge: Purge the context-mode knowledge base. Permanently deletes all indexed content\nand resets session stats. This is destructive and cannot be undone.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-purge\n- context-mode:ctx-search: Search context-mode's persistent FTS5 knowledge base for previously indexed\nlocal project content, documentation, or session memory.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-search\n- context-mode:ctx-stats: Show how much context window context-mode saved this session.\nDisplays token consumption, context savings ratio, and per-tool breakdown.\nRead-only — shows stats only, no reset capability.\nTo wipe the knowledge base entirely, use ctx_purge instead.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-stats\n- context-mode:ctx-upgrade: Update context-mode from GitHub and fix hooks/settings.\nPulls latest, builds, installs, updates npm global, configures hooks.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-upgrade\n- codex:codex-cli-runtime: Internal helper contract for calling the codex-companion runtime from Claude Code\n- codex:codex-result-handling: Internal guidance for presenting Codex helper output back to the user\n- codex:gpt-5-4-prompting: Internal guidance for composing Codex and GPT-5.4 prompts for coding, review, diagnosis, and research tasks inside the Codex Claude Code plugin\n- mattpocock-skills:diagnosing-bugs: Diagnosis loop for hard bugs and performance regressions. Use when the user says \"diagnose\"/\"debug this\", or reports something broken/throwing/failing/slow.\n- mattpocock-skills:tdd: Test-driven development. Use when the user wants to build features or fix bugs test-first, mentions \"red-green-refactor\", or wants integration tests.\n- mattpocock-skills:prototype: Build a throwaway prototype to answer a design question. Use when the user wants to sanity-check whether a state model or logic feels right, or explore what a UI should look like.\n- mattpocock-skills:research: Investigate a question against high-trust primary sources and capture the findings as a Markdown file in the repo. Use when the user wants a topic researched, docs or API facts gathered, or reading legwork delegated to a background agent.\n- mattpocock-skills:domain-modeling: Build and sharpen a project's domain model. Use when the user wants to pin down domain terminology or a ubiquitous language, record an architectural decision, or when another skill needs to maintain the domain model.\n- mattpocock-skills:codebase-design: Shared vocabulary for designing deep modules. Use when the user wants to design or improve a module's interface, find deepening opportunities, decide where a seam goes, make code more testable or AI-navigable, or when another skill needs the deep-module vocabulary.\n- mattpocock-skills:code-review: Review the changes since a fixed point (commit, branch, tag, or merge-base) along two axes — Standards (does the code follow this repo's documented coding standards?) and Spec (does the code match what the originating issue/spec asked for?). Runs both reviews in parallel sub-agents and reports them side by side. Use when the user wants to review a branch, a PR, work-in-progress changes, or asks to \"review since X\".\n- mattpocock-skills:resolving-merge-conflicts: Use when you need to resolve an in-progress git merge/rebase conflict.\n- mattpocock-skills:wizard: Generate an interactive bash wizard that walks a human through steps only they can perform. Use when provisioning infrastructure, setting up credentials or CI secrets, walking an unfamiliar third-party dashboard, or running a one-off migration or cutover. Don't invoke this for steps the agent can perform itself.\n- mattpocock-skills:grilling: Grill the user relentlessly about a plan, decision, or idea. Use when the user wants to stress-test their thinking, or uses any 'grill' trigger phrases.\n- mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents: Writing documents for agents. Use when creating or editing skills, or modifying AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md.\n- watch:watch: Watch a video (URL or local path). Downloads with yt-dlp, extracts auto-scaled frames with ffmpeg, pulls the transcript from captions (or Whisper API fallback), and hands the result to Claude so it can answer questions about what's in the video.\n- cowork-plugin-management:cowork-plugin-customizer: Customize a Claude Code plugin for a specific organization's tools and workflows. Use when: customize plugin, set up plugin, configure plugin, tailor plugin, adjust plugin settings, customize plugin connectors, customize plugin skill, tweak plugin, modify plugin configuration.\n- cowork-plugin-management:create-cowork-plugin: Guide users through creating a new plugin from scratch in a cowork session. Use when users want to create a plugin, build a plugin, make a new plugin, develop a plugin, scaffold a plugin, start a plugin from scratch, or design a plugin. This skill requires Cowork mode with access to the outputs directory for delivering the final .plugin file.\n- desktop-commander:ai-tools-setup: Set up, connect, validate, and repair Claude Desktop and MCP servers using Desktop Commander — inspecting and fixing mcp.json / claude_desktop_config.json, getting MCP servers to connect, and diagnosing local AI tooling. Use when a user wants to install, configure, connect, validate, or fix Claude Desktop, an MCP server, an MCP config file, or a local AI agent/gateway. Trigger on the symptoms that actually bring people in: \"Claude can't see my tools\", \"my MCP server isn't showing up\", \"tools aren't loading in Claude\", \"add an MCP server\", \"my MCP isn't connecting\", \"fix my Claude Desktop config\", \"the agent won't respond\", \"wire up Ollama / LM Studio\", \"restart Claude Desktop\". Also covers local agent gateways like OpenClaw and Hermes.\n- desktop-commander:computer-health-check: Run a comprehensive, read-only health check on the user's computer and return a scored chat summary with prioritized, plain-English recommendations and safe cleanup suggestions. Use this whenever the user wants to check their computer's health, speed it up, free up / reclaim disk space, find what's eating CPU / memory / storage, check battery health and wear, audit login & startup items, see pending updates, or asks for a \"tune-up\", \"checkup\", \"system report\", \"health check\", or \"is my Mac/PC/laptop healthy\". Trigger even on casual phrasing like \"my laptop feels slow\", \"why is my fan so loud\", \"my computer is laggy\", \"running out of space\", or \"clean up my machine\". Works on macOS, Windows, and Linux. This is a Desktop Commander skill: it relies on a real local shell (start_process / interact_with_process) and is strictly read-only by default — it never needs sudo and only performs cleanups the user explicitly approves.\n- desktop-commander:desktop-commander-overview: Use for Desktop Commander MCP capabilities — persistent shells and REPLs, long-running processes, filesystem beyond the workspace, structured files (.xlsx, .docx, .pdf, images) and large local data files such as CSVs, ripgrep search at scale, SSH, or cross-turn state.\n- desktop-commander:knowledge-base: Create and maintain a Markdown knowledge base that any AI agent can read, search, and update. Use when the user wants to start a knowledge base, add or update notes, organize docs/notes for an agent or LLM to consume, build an index of notes, or run a cleanup/maintenance pass on an existing MD knowledge base. Triggers include \"knowledge base\", \"KB\", \"notes for the agent\", \"index of notes\", \"second brain\", \"docs for AI context\", \"add a note\", \"update the KB\".\n- desktop-commander:obsidian-vault: Organize Obsidian vaults with MOCs, wikilinks, frontmatter/properties, dashboards, orphan-note checks, and cleanup workflows. Use ONLY when the user explicitly refers to Obsidian — by naming \"Obsidian\", an Obsidian \"vault\", or an Obsidian-specific feature such as wikilinks (\"[[ ]]\"), the Dataview or Bases plugins, or Obsidian Properties. Within that Obsidian context it covers creating Maps of Content, adding/fixing wikilinks, finding orphan notes, normalizing YAML frontmatter, generating Dataview/Bases dashboards, organizing folders, deduplicating or renaming notes, and preparing the vault for AI use. Do NOT use this skill for generic note-taking, Markdown, or knowledge-base requests that don't mention Obsidian.\n- desktop-commander:terminal: Use Desktop Commander for terminal and command-line work, especially anything that needs a shell whose state persists across turns: Python/Node REPLs, database shells, dev servers and other long-running processes, SSH into remote machines, and Windows PowerShell. Also handles everyday terminal tasks — navigating folders, choosing the right command for the user's shell (PowerShell, cmd, bash, zsh), running Docker/curl/cloud-CLI commands, inspecting processes and ports, and saving recurring workflows as scripts — even when the user doesn't say \"Desktop Commander\" or \"terminal.\" Reach for it on pasted errors like \"command not found\", \"permission denied\", \"EADDRINUSE\", \"address already in use\", \"npm ERR!\", \"ModuleNotFoundError\", or \"ENOENT\", and on intents like \"what's using port 3000\", \"kill that process\", \"ssh into my server\", or \"why won't my dev server start\". Works on Windows, macOS, and Linux. Never run destructive commands without explicit confirmation.\n- firecrawl:firecrawl: Any live-web task via the Firecrawl CLI — including ordinary web research: searching the web, reading or extracting pages, gathering sources, discovering site URLs, bulk extraction, downloading a site, change alerts, or pages needing clicks/login — web only; local files route to firecrawl-parse. For papers use firecrawl-research-index; for library, API, error, or bug questions use firecrawl-developer-index.\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-agent: Autonomous multi-page extraction into structured JSON. Use when the user wants website data matching a schema — pricing tiers, product listings — beyond a single-page scrape.\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-crawl\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-developer-index\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-download\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-interact\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-map\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-monitor\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-parse\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-research-index\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-scrape\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-search\n- exa:exa-agent\n- exa:search\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. When the destination is a first-party document connector (host-designated, never self-described) that renders live charts, hand it the rows (inline, or as an uploaded data file the chart cites) rather than a rendered PNG/SVG — a picture of a chart loses hover, data inspection and per-value comments. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- artifact-design: Design guidance and fundamentals for Artifacts. - Load before writing any artifact, including a skill-instructed Markdown one - Markdown is never a shortcut past the design pass.\n- artifact-diagramming: Diagramming know-how for Artifacts - when a picture earns its place, how to draw one that shows the real mechanism, and the inline-SVG mechanics that keep it legible in both themes.\n- artifact-capabilities: Runtime capabilities a published Artifact page can be granted — behavior static HTML cannot provide on its own, such as the page reading live or connected data, remembering what people do on it (a poll, a sign-up sheet, a checklist, a document edited in place — it saves new versions of itself), keeping state shared across viewers, knowing who is viewing, asking Claude a question of its own, storing files people add, or handing the viewer a file to save. Serves this user's live capability roster and the typed call definitions. Load it whenever any such runtime behavior would make an artifact more useful, before writing the page.\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- schedule: Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule. - When the user wants to schedule a recurring cloud agent, set up automated tasks, create a cron job for Claude Code, or manage their scheduled agents/routines. Also use when the user wants a one-time scheduled run (\"run this once at 3pm\", \"remind me to check X tomorrow\").\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- workflow-authoring: Reference for writing a Workflow tool script (script API and gotchas, resume, quality patterns, worked examples). Load before authoring a script for a workflow the user already opted into; it does not itself authorize running one.\n- claude-in-chrome: Automates your Chrome browser to interact with web pages - clicking elements, filling forms, capturing screenshots, reading console logs, and navigating sites. Opens pages in new tabs within your existing Chrome session. Requires site-level permissions before executing (configured in the extension). - When the user wants to interact with web pages, automate browser tasks, capture screenshots, read console logs, or perform any browser-based actions. Always invoke BEFORE attempting to use any mcp__claude-in-chrome__* tools.\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init: Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation\n- security-review\n- anthropic-skills:closeout\n- anthropic-skills:dev-sync\n- anthropic-skills:docs\n- anthropic-skills:docx\n- anthropic-skills:five-slides-generator\n- anthropic-skills:humanizer\n- anthropic-skills:import-memory\n- anthropic-skills:last30days\n- anthropic-skills:mcp-builder\n- anthropic-skills:migrate-project-to-disk\n- anthropic-skills:morning\n- anthropic-skills:new-project\n- anthropic-skills:pdf\n- anthropic-skills:pptx\n- anthropic-skills:skill-creator\n- anthropic-skills:slide-deck-generator\n- anthropic-skills:watch\n- anthropic-skills:xlsx","skillCount":147,"isInitial":true,"names":["anti-sleep","ArXiv","backup","brain-audit","capture-insight","claude-stack-doctor","claudex-loop","claudex-route","cli-hub-meta-skill","closeout","code-review","codex-build","codex-review","CreateSkill","design-taste-frontend","dev-sync","doctor-plus","fireflies-intake","five-slides-generator","forecast-capture","graphify","harvest","html-to-pdf","impeccable","improve-system","ingest-book","ingest-resource","interview-me","ISA","library-advisor","library-review","meeting-prep","meeting-sync","memory-write","name-meetings","new-project","next-decision","pbir-report-builder","persistent-localhost","pp-catapult-openfield-connect","pp-zotero-research-library","project","project-triage","Science","scroll-craft","session-miner","sports-science-research-grounding","startup","status-report","weekly-review","codex:rescue","codex:setup","firecrawl:skill-gen","superpowers:brainstorming","superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents","superpowers:executing-plans","superpowers:finishing-a-development-branch","superpowers:receiving-code-review","superpowers:requesting-code-review","superpowers:subagent-driven-development","superpowers:systematic-debugging","superpowers:test-driven-development","superpowers:using-git-worktrees","superpowers:using-superpowers","superpowers:verification-before-completion","superpowers:writing-plans","superpowers:writing-skills","last30days:last30days","context-mode:context-mode","context-mode:ctx-doctor","context-mode:ctx-index","context-mode:ctx-insight","context-mode:ctx-purge","context-mode:ctx-search","context-mode:ctx-stats","context-mode:ctx-upgrade","codex:codex-cli-runtime","codex:codex-result-handling","codex:gpt-5-4-prompting","mattpocock-skills:diagnosing-bugs","mattpocock-skills:tdd","mattpocock-skills:prototype","mattpocock-skills:research","mattpocock-skills:domain-modeling","mattpocock-skills:codebase-design","mattpocock-skills:code-review","mattpocock-skills:resolving-merge-conflicts","mattpocock-skills:wizard","mattpocock-skills:grilling","mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents","watch:watch","cowork-plugin-management:cowork-plugin-customizer","cowork-plugin-management:create-cowork-plugin","desktop-commander:ai-tools-setup","desktop-commander:computer-health-check","desktop-commander:desktop-commander-overview","desktop-commander:knowledge-base","desktop-commander:obsidian-vault","desktop-commander:terminal","firecrawl:firecrawl","firecrawl:firecrawl-agent","firecrawl:firecrawl-crawl","firecrawl:firecrawl-developer-index","firecrawl:firecrawl-download","firecrawl:firecrawl-interact","firecrawl:firecrawl-map","firecrawl:firecrawl-monitor","firecrawl:firecrawl-parse","firecrawl:firecrawl-research-index","firecrawl:firecrawl-scrape","firecrawl:firecrawl-search","exa:exa-agent","exa:search","dataviz","artifact-design","artifact-diagramming","artifact-capabilities","update-config","keybindings-help","simplify","fewer-permission-prompts","loop","schedule","claude-api","workflow-authoring","claude-in-chrome","run","init","security-review","anthropic-skills:closeout","anthropic-skills:dev-sync","anthropic-skills:docs","anthropic-skills:docx","anthropic-skills:five-slides-generator","anthropic-skills:humanizer","anthropic-skills:import-memory","anthropic-skills:last30days","anthropic-skills:mcp-builder","anthropic-skills:migrate-project-to-disk","anthropic-skills:morning","anthropic-skills:new-project","anthropic-skills:pdf","anthropic-skills:pptx","anthropic-skills:skill-creator","anthropic-skills:slide-deck-generator","anthropic-skills:watch","anthropic-skills:xlsx"]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"7fd2eaae-7293-4847-8b96-14993565b531","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:43:26.602Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nThe following skills are available for use with the Skill tool:\n\n- anti-sleep\n- ArXiv\n- backup\n- brain-audit\n- capture-insight\n- claude-stack-doctor: Health-check Eric's automation/memory stack in one pass and verify the Mac-to-Windows handoff works: launchd agents, Python venvs, memsearch, the memory-compiler flush pipeline, backup freshness, and context budget. Use when the user says \"/claude-stack-doctor\", \"stack doctor\", \"is my stack healthy\", \"did my scheduled jobs run\", \"check the memory pipeline\", \"why didn't the backup run\", or when closeout/morning sessions should verify automation health. Do not use to run the backup itself (use backup) or to audit context-engineering practice (use doctor-plus).\n- claudex-loop\n- claudex-route\n- cli-hub-meta-skill\n- closeout\n- code-review\n- codex-build\n- codex-review: Have Codex independently review an existing implementation plan while Claude coordinates a bounded revision loop. For automatic reviewer selection from either host, use claudex-loop with mode=review.\n- CreateSkill: Invoke BEFORE any skill work — creating, modifying, adding a workflow/tool to, renaming, validating, or canonicalizing any skill. Required by skills/CLAUDE.md; the single home for skill authoring in this workspace. Owns the skill lifecycle: scaffold (public TitleCase vs private _ALLCAPS, flat 2-level, Gotchas + Examples, BPE check), validate, canonicalize, test, improve, optimize descriptions. USE WHEN create skill, new skill, make a skill, build a skill, set up a skill, private skill, make a X skill, add a workflow, add a tool, edit/change/update/rename a skill, skill frontmatter, validate skill, check skill, canonicalize, scaffold skill, test skill, improve skill, optimize description, skill not triggering, overtriggering. NOT FOR TypeScript CLI generation (use CreateCLI).\n- design-taste-frontend\n- dev-sync\n- doctor-plus\n- fireflies-intake: One-command Fireflies pipeline — sync new meeting transcripts into the SecondBrain vault, rename generically-titled meetings, then report exactly what was ingested. Use when the user says \"/fireflies-intake\", \"ingest my recent fireflies meetings\", \"sync meetings that aren't in the second brain yet\", \"pull in the new meetings\", \"meeting ingestion\", or asks whether all recent meetings have been ingested. Do not use to search what was said in transcripts (use pp-fireflies).\n- five-slides-generator\n- forecast-capture\n- graphify: Use for any question about a codebase, its architecture, file relationships, or project content — especially when graphify-out/ exists, where the question should be treated as a graphify query first. Turns any input (code, docs, papers, images, videos) into a persistent knowledge graph with god nodes, community detection, and query/path/explain tools. Do not use for a quick lookup of one known file or symbol, or for sport-science literature questions (use sports-science-research-grounding).\n- harvest\n- html-to-pdf: Render any local HTML report, deck, or dashboard to a print-ready PDF via headless Chrome — replaces the manual Ctrl+P → Save as PDF ritual. Use when the user says \"export this to PDF\", \"print version\", \"PDF for the coaches\", \"make this printable\", or a coach-facing HTML deliverable needs a shareable/printable copy. Do not use to build the report or deck itself (use status-report or five-slides-generator).\n- impeccable: Use when the user wants to design, redesign, shape, critique, audit, polish, clarify, distill, harden, optimize, adapt, animate, colorize, extract, or otherwise improve a frontend interface. Covers websites, landing pages, dashboards, product UI, app shells, components, forms, settings, onboarding, and empty states. Handles UX review, visual hierarchy, information architecture, cognitive load, accessibility, performance, responsive behavior, theming, anti-patterns, typography, fonts, spacing, layout, alignment, color, motion, micro-interactions, UX copy, error states, edge cases, i18n, and reusable design systems or tokens. Also use for bland designs that need to become bolder or more delightful, loud designs that should become quieter, live browser iteration on UI elements, or ambitious visual effects that should feel technically extraordinary. Not for backend-only or non-UI tasks.\n- improve-system\n- ingest-book\n- ingest-resource\n- interview-me\n- ISA\n- library-advisor\n- library-review\n- meeting-prep: Pre-meeting readiness brief for a project — what changed, what's open, every deliverable path. Use when the user says \"/meeting-prep <project>\", \"I have a meeting with <person> — are we in a good spot on <project>?\", \"verify we're up to speed before the meeting\", \"get me ready for this afternoon's meeting\", or names an upcoming coach/staff meeting. Do not use for the biweekly supervisor report (use status-report) or to sync meeting transcripts (use meeting-sync).\n- meeting-sync\n- memory-write: Writes, updates, removes, and recalls facts in the two-tier memory system (~/.claude/global-memory/ and ~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/). Use for write requests such as \"remember this\", \"note that\", \"update memory\", \"save this\", and \"forget about\", and for recall requests such as \"did we already…?\", \"what did we decide about X?\", and \"do you remember…?\". Picks the tier, dedups, and enforces caps; recall checks the injected snapshot, then memsearch. Do not use for a story or lesson worth a vault note (use capture-insight) or an article to keep (use ingest-resource).\n- name-meetings: Detects Fireflies meetings with generic titles (empty, \"Zoom Meeting\", \"Recording\", date-only, etc.), proposes structured names based on AI summary + participants, and applies approved renames via the Fireflies API. Use when user says \"/name-meetings\", \"name my meetings\", \"rename untitled meetings\", \"fix meeting titles\", \"my meetings have no names\", or when invoked by meeting-sync. Do not use to sync transcripts or audit vault filenames (use meeting-sync).\n- new-project\n- next-decision\n- pbir-report-builder\n- persistent-localhost\n- pp-catapult-openfield-connect\n- pp-zotero-research-library\n- project: Manage the portfolio project registry (ideas, updates, ranking, promotion, dev stages) and render/publish the dashboard. Use when the user says \"/project\", \"new idea:\", \"log progress on\", \"project update\", \"add to my project inbox\", \"show my projects\", \"re-rank my projects\", \"set the stage on\", \"mark <project> as spec'd/planned/shipped\". Do not use for the DEV drift dashboard (use project-triage) or to scaffold a new repo (use new-project).\n- project-triage\n- Science\n- scroll-craft\n- session-miner\n- sports-science-research-grounding: Use when the user asks a substantive question about sport science, return to play or sport, rehabilitation, strength and conditioning, workload, recovery, testing, athlete health, injury, performance, or wants a research-backed answer, and for Zotero full-library batch processing. Searches the user's Zotero library first, clearly labels external peer-reviewed fallback sources, and applies tier-aware model routing during bounded batch work. Do not use for sports scores or schedules (use pp-espn) or for a simple Zotero search or sync (use pp-zotero-research-library).\n- startup\n- status-report: Build the every-two-weeks Applied Performance status report for Kenny Boyd and Chris Ruf — paste-ready email, one-page PDF, internal record. Use when the user says \"/status-report\", \"build my status report\", \"time for the Chris report\", \"biweekly update\", \"status update for Chris and Kenny\", \"what do I send Chris this week\", or when a scheduled report date arrives. Do not use for a single meeting brief (use meeting-prep) or the end-of-day supervisor log interview (use interview-me).\n- weekly-review\n- codex:rescue: Delegate investigation, an explicit fix request, or follow-up rescue work to the Codex rescue subagent\n- codex:setup: Check whether the local Codex CLI is ready and optionally toggle the stop-time review gate\n- firecrawl:skill-gen: Generate a complete Agent Skill from a documentation URL using Firecrawl\n- superpowers:brainstorming: You MUST use this before any creative work - creating features, building components, adding functionality, or modifying behavior. Explores user intent, requirements and design before implementation.\n- superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents: Use when facing 2+ independent tasks that can be worked on without shared state or sequential dependencies\n- superpowers:executing-plans: Use when you have a written implementation plan to execute in a separate session with review checkpoints\n- superpowers:finishing-a-development-branch: Use when implementation is complete, all tests pass, and you need to decide how to integrate the work\n- superpowers:receiving-code-review: Use when receiving code review feedback, before implementing suggestions, especially if feedback seems unclear or technically questionable - requires technical rigor and verification, not performative agreement or blind implementation\n- superpowers:requesting-code-review: Use when completing tasks, implementing major features, or before merging to verify work meets requirements\n- superpowers:subagent-driven-development: Use when executing implementation plans with independent tasks in the current session\n- superpowers:systematic-debugging: Use when encountering any bug, test failure, or unexpected behavior, before proposing fixes\n- superpowers:test-driven-development: Use when implementing any feature or bugfix, before writing implementation code\n- superpowers:using-git-worktrees: Use when starting feature work that needs isolation from current workspace or before executing implementation plans - ensures an isolated workspace exists via native tools or git worktree fallback\n- superpowers:using-superpowers: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n- superpowers:verification-before-completion: Use when about to claim work is complete, fixed, or passing, before committing or creating PRs - requires running verification commands and confirming output before making any success claims; evidence before assertions always\n- superpowers:writing-plans: Use when you have a spec or requirements for a multi-step task, before touching code\n- superpowers:writing-skills: Use when creating new skills, editing existing skills, or verifying skills work before deployment\n- last30days:last30days: Research what people actually say about any topic in the last 30 days. Pulls posts and engagement from Reddit, X, YouTube, TikTok, Hacker News, Polymarket, GitHub, and the web. Includes a doctor health check to diagnose broken or missing sources.\n- context-mode:context-mode: Use context-mode tools (ctx_execute, ctx_execute_file) instead of Bash/cat when processing\nlarge outputs. Triggers: \"analyze logs\", \"summarize output\", \"process data\",\n\"parse JSON\", \"filter results\", \"extract errors\", \"check build output\",\n\"analyze dependencies\", \"process API response\", \"large file analysis\",\n\"page snapshot\", \"browser snapshot\", \"DOM structure\", \"inspect page\",\n\"accessibility tree\", \"Playwright snapshot\",\n\"run tests\", \"test output\", \"coverage report\", \"git log\", \"recent commits\",\n\"diff between branches\", \"list containers\", \"pod status\", \"disk usage\",\n\"fetch docs\", \"API reference\", \"index documentation\",\n\"call API\", \"check response\", \"query results\",\n\"find TODOs\", \"count lines\", \"codebase statistics\", \"security audit\",\n\"outdated packages\", \"dependency tree\", \"cloud resources\", \"CI/CD output\".\nAlso triggers on ANY MCP tool output that may exceed 20 lines.\nSubagent routing is handled automatically via PreToolUse hook.\n- context-mode:ctx-doctor: Run context-mode diagnostics. Checks runtimes, hooks, FTS5,\nplugin registration, npm and marketplace versions.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-doctor\n- context-mode:ctx-index: Index a local file or directory into context-mode's persistent FTS5 knowledge base\nso future ctx_search calls can retrieve focused snippets without rereading raw files.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-index\n- context-mode:ctx-insight: Open the context-mode Insight dashboard in your default browser.\nInsight is the hosted analytics layer for AI-assisted engineering teams —\nper-engineer productive rate, retry waste, blocker detection, role-narrowed views.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-insight\n- context-mode:ctx-purge: Purge the context-mode knowledge base. Permanently deletes all indexed content\nand resets session stats. This is destructive and cannot be undone.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-purge\n- context-mode:ctx-search: Search context-mode's persistent FTS5 knowledge base for previously indexed\nlocal project content, documentation, or session memory.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-search\n- context-mode:ctx-stats: Show how much context window context-mode saved this session.\nDisplays token consumption, context savings ratio, and per-tool breakdown.\nRead-only — shows stats only, no reset capability.\nTo wipe the knowledge base entirely, use ctx_purge instead.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-stats\n- context-mode:ctx-upgrade: Update context-mode from GitHub and fix hooks/settings.\nPulls latest, builds, installs, updates npm global, configures hooks.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-upgrade\n- codex:codex-cli-runtime: Internal helper contract for calling the codex-companion runtime from Claude Code\n- codex:codex-result-handling: Internal guidance for presenting Codex helper output back to the user\n- codex:gpt-5-4-prompting: Internal guidance for composing Codex and GPT-5.4 prompts for coding, review, diagnosis, and research tasks inside the Codex Claude Code plugin\n- mattpocock-skills:diagnosing-bugs: Diagnosis loop for hard bugs and performance regressions. Use when the user says \"diagnose\"/\"debug this\", or reports something broken/throwing/failing/slow.\n- mattpocock-skills:tdd: Test-driven development. Use when the user wants to build features or fix bugs test-first, mentions \"red-green-refactor\", or wants integration tests.\n- mattpocock-skills:prototype: Build a throwaway prototype to answer a design question. Use when the user wants to sanity-check whether a state model or logic feels right, or explore what a UI should look like.\n- mattpocock-skills:research: Investigate a question against high-trust primary sources and capture the findings as a Markdown file in the repo. Use when the user wants a topic researched, docs or API facts gathered, or reading legwork delegated to a background agent.\n- mattpocock-skills:domain-modeling: Build and sharpen a project's domain model. Use when the user wants to pin down domain terminology or a ubiquitous language, record an architectural decision, or when another skill needs to maintain the domain model.\n- mattpocock-skills:codebase-design: Shared vocabulary for designing deep modules. Use when the user wants to design or improve a module's interface, find deepening opportunities, decide where a seam goes, make code more testable or AI-navigable, or when another skill needs the deep-module vocabulary.\n- mattpocock-skills:code-review: Review the changes since a fixed point (commit, branch, tag, or merge-base) along two axes — Standards (does the code follow this repo's documented coding standards?) and Spec (does the code match what the originating issue/spec asked for?). Runs both reviews in parallel sub-agents and reports them side by side. Use when the user wants to review a branch, a PR, work-in-progress changes, or asks to \"review since X\".\n- mattpocock-skills:resolving-merge-conflicts: Use when you need to resolve an in-progress git merge/rebase conflict.\n- mattpocock-skills:wizard: Generate an interactive bash wizard that walks a human through steps only they can perform. Use when provisioning infrastructure, setting up credentials or CI secrets, walking an unfamiliar third-party dashboard, or running a one-off migration or cutover. Don't invoke this for steps the agent can perform itself.\n- mattpocock-skills:grilling: Grill the user relentlessly about a plan, decision, or idea. Use when the user wants to stress-test their thinking, or uses any 'grill' trigger phrases.\n- mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents: Writing documents for agents. Use when creating or editing skills, or modifying AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md.\n- watch:watch: Watch a video (URL or local path). Downloads with yt-dlp, extracts auto-scaled frames with ffmpeg, pulls the transcript from captions (or Whisper API fallback), and hands the result to Claude so it can answer questions about what's in the video.\n- cowork-plugin-management:cowork-plugin-customizer: Customize a Claude Code plugin for a specific organization's tools and workflows. Use when: customize plugin, set up plugin, configure plugin, tailor plugin, adjust plugin settings, customize plugin connectors, customize plugin skill, tweak plugin, modify plugin configuration.\n- cowork-plugin-management:create-cowork-plugin: Guide users through creating a new plugin from scratch in a cowork session. Use when users want to create a plugin, build a plugin, make a new plugin, develop a plugin, scaffold a plugin, start a plugin from scratch, or design a plugin. This skill requires Cowork mode with access to the outputs directory for delivering the final .plugin file.\n- desktop-commander:ai-tools-setup: Set up, connect, validate, and repair Claude Desktop and MCP servers using Desktop Commander — inspecting and fixing mcp.json / claude_desktop_config.json, getting MCP servers to connect, and diagnosing local AI tooling. Use when a user wants to install, configure, connect, validate, or fix Claude Desktop, an MCP server, an MCP config file, or a local AI agent/gateway. Trigger on the symptoms that actually bring people in: \"Claude can't see my tools\", \"my MCP server isn't showing up\", \"tools aren't loading in Claude\", \"add an MCP server\", \"my MCP isn't connecting\", \"fix my Claude Desktop config\", \"the agent won't respond\", \"wire up Ollama / LM Studio\", \"restart Claude Desktop\". Also covers local agent gateways like OpenClaw and Hermes.\n- desktop-commander:computer-health-check: Run a comprehensive, read-only health check on the user's computer and return a scored chat summary with prioritized, plain-English recommendations and safe cleanup suggestions. Use this whenever the user wants to check their computer's health, speed it up, free up / reclaim disk space, find what's eating CPU / memory / storage, check battery health and wear, audit login & startup items, see pending updates, or asks for a \"tune-up\", \"checkup\", \"system report\", \"health check\", or \"is my Mac/PC/laptop healthy\". Trigger even on casual phrasing like \"my laptop feels slow\", \"why is my fan so loud\", \"my computer is laggy\", \"running out of space\", or \"clean up my machine\". Works on macOS, Windows, and Linux. This is a Desktop Commander skill: it relies on a real local shell (start_process / interact_with_process) and is strictly read-only by default — it never needs sudo and only performs cleanups the user explicitly approves.\n- desktop-commander:desktop-commander-overview: Use for Desktop Commander MCP capabilities — persistent shells and REPLs, long-running processes, filesystem beyond the workspace, structured files (.xlsx, .docx, .pdf, images) and large local data files such as CSVs, ripgrep search at scale, SSH, or cross-turn state.\n- desktop-commander:knowledge-base: Create and maintain a Markdown knowledge base that any AI agent can read, search, and update. Use when the user wants to start a knowledge base, add or update notes, organize docs/notes for an agent or LLM to consume, build an index of notes, or run a cleanup/maintenance pass on an existing MD knowledge base. Triggers include \"knowledge base\", \"KB\", \"notes for the agent\", \"index of notes\", \"second brain\", \"docs for AI context\", \"add a note\", \"update the KB\".\n- desktop-commander:obsidian-vault: Organize Obsidian vaults with MOCs, wikilinks, frontmatter/properties, dashboards, orphan-note checks, and cleanup workflows. Use ONLY when the user explicitly refers to Obsidian — by naming \"Obsidian\", an Obsidian \"vault\", or an Obsidian-specific feature such as wikilinks (\"[[ ]]\"), the Dataview or Bases plugins, or Obsidian Properties. Within that Obsidian context it covers creating Maps of Content, adding/fixing wikilinks, finding orphan notes, normalizing YAML frontmatter, generating Dataview/Bases dashboards, organizing folders, deduplicating or renaming notes, and preparing the vault for AI use. Do NOT use this skill for generic note-taking, Markdown, or knowledge-base requests that don't mention Obsidian.\n- desktop-commander:terminal: Use Desktop Commander for terminal and command-line work, especially anything that needs a shell whose state persists across turns: Python/Node REPLs, database shells, dev servers and other long-running processes, SSH into remote machines, and Windows PowerShell. Also handles everyday terminal tasks — navigating folders, choosing the right command for the user's shell (PowerShell, cmd, bash, zsh), running Docker/curl/cloud-CLI commands, inspecting processes and ports, and saving recurring workflows as scripts — even when the user doesn't say \"Desktop Commander\" or \"terminal.\" Reach for it on pasted errors like \"command not found\", \"permission denied\", \"EADDRINUSE\", \"address already in use\", \"npm ERR!\", \"ModuleNotFoundError\", or \"ENOENT\", and on intents like \"what's using port 3000\", \"kill that process\", \"ssh into my server\", or \"why won't my dev server start\". Works on Windows, macOS, and Linux. Never run destructive commands without explicit confirmation.\n- firecrawl:firecrawl: Any live-web task via the Firecrawl CLI — including ordinary web research: searching the web, reading or extracting pages, gathering sources, discovering site URLs, bulk extraction, downloading a site, change alerts, or pages needing clicks/login — web only; local files route to firecrawl-parse. For papers use firecrawl-research-index; for library, API, error, or bug questions use firecrawl-developer-index.\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-agent: Autonomous multi-page extraction into structured JSON. Use when the user wants website data matching a schema — pricing tiers, product listings — beyond a single-page scrape.\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-crawl\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-developer-index\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-download\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-interact\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-map\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-monitor\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-parse\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-research-index\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-scrape\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-search\n- exa:exa-agent\n- exa:search\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. When the destination is a first-party document connector (host-designated, never self-described) that renders live charts, hand it the rows (inline, or as an uploaded data file the chart cites) rather than a rendered PNG/SVG — a picture of a chart loses hover, data inspection and per-value comments. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- artifact-design: Design guidance and fundamentals for Artifacts. - Load before writing any artifact, including a skill-instructed Markdown one - Markdown is never a shortcut past the design pass.\n- artifact-diagramming: Diagramming know-how for Artifacts - when a picture earns its place, how to draw one that shows the real mechanism, and the inline-SVG mechanics that keep it legible in both themes.\n- artifact-capabilities: Runtime capabilities a published Artifact page can be granted — behavior static HTML cannot provide on its own, such as the page reading live or connected data, remembering what people do on it (a poll, a sign-up sheet, a checklist, a document edited in place — it saves new versions of itself), keeping state shared across viewers, knowing who is viewing, asking Claude a question of its own, storing files people add, or handing the viewer a file to save. Serves this user's live capability roster and the typed call definitions. Load it whenever any such runtime behavior would make an artifact more useful, before writing the page.\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- schedule: Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule. - When the user wants to schedule a recurring cloud agent, set up automated tasks, create a cron job for Claude Code, or manage their scheduled agents/routines. Also use when the user wants a one-time scheduled run (\"run this once at 3pm\", \"remind me to check X tomorrow\").\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- workflow-authoring: Reference for writing a Workflow tool script (script API and gotchas, resume, quality patterns, worked examples). Load before authoring a script for a workflow the user already opted into; it does not itself authorize running one.\n- claude-in-chrome: Automates your Chrome browser to interact with web pages - clicking elements, filling forms, capturing screenshots, reading console logs, and navigating sites. Opens pages in new tabs within your existing Chrome session. Requires site-level permissions before executing (configured in the extension). - When the user wants to interact with web pages, automate browser tasks, capture screenshots, read console logs, or perform any browser-based actions. Always invoke BEFORE attempting to use any mcp__claude-in-chrome__* tools.\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init: Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation\n- security-review\n- anthropic-skills:closeout\n- anthropic-skills:dev-sync\n- anthropic-skills:docs\n- anthropic-skills:docx\n- anthropic-skills:five-slides-generator\n- anthropic-skills:humanizer\n- anthropic-skills:import-memory\n- anthropic-skills:last30days\n- anthropic-skills:mcp-builder\n- anthropic-skills:migrate-project-to-disk\n- anthropic-skills:morning\n- anthropic-skills:new-project\n- anthropic-skills:pdf\n- anthropic-skills:pptx\n- anthropic-skills:skill-creator\n- anthropic-skills:slide-deck-generator\n- anthropic-skills:watch\n- anthropic-skills:xlsx\n</system-reminder>"}],"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"35d4f785-606b-402b-829e-4e9c3081934b","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"7fd2eaae-7293-4847-8b96-14993565b531","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"auto_mode","autoModeConsentFlow":false,"bashFirst":true,"bashFirstSteer":"relaxed","steerOnly":true,"bypass":false},"type":"attachment","uuid":"12fa785c-738b-49e1-bb38-b2be1f79655b","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:43:26.602Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nWhile auto mode is active:\n\nYou can do much of your work through the Bash tool when it is the simpler route: read files with cat, head, or sed -n, search with grep and find, and make small, mechanical file changes with sed, heredocs, or short scripts instead of the dedicated Read, Edit, or Write tools. The choice is yours: prefer Edit or Write when a shell edit would be fragile, such as exact or multi-line replacements, or sed/awk flags that differ between GNU and BSD/macOS.\n</system-reminder>"}],"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"35d4f785-606b-402b-829e-4e9c3081934b","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"12fa785c-738b-49e1-bb38-b2be1f79655b","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>15000000 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"7bcec6e4-923f-4621-a79d-d99aa07e4a00","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:43:26.602Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>15000000 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"35d4f785-606b-402b-829e-4e9c3081934b","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"7bcec6e4-923f-4621-a79d-d99aa07e4a00","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"output_style","style":"ELI5"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"a11d9c76-8bfa-4910-aefd-4842e5bac153","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:43:26.602Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nELI5 output style is active. Remember to follow the specific guidelines for this style.\n</system-reminder>"}],"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"35d4f785-606b-402b-829e-4e9c3081934b","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"a11d9c76-8bfa-4910-aefd-4842e5bac153","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"instructions","files":[{"path":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/CLAUDE.md","type":"User","content":"# Machine\n\nMachine-specific shell, paths, and traps live in `~/.claude/machine.md` (gitignored; copy `machine.macos.md` or `machine.windows.md` to `machine.md` on a new machine). It is imported below.\n\n@machine.md\n\nTwo machines share this setup: the MacBook (`~/DEV`) and the Baylor Windows laptop (`~/Desktop/DEV`). Projects sync through GitHub (`erash11`), documents through Google Drive. Every project repo carries a Session routine in its `AGENTS.md`: `git pull` + read `docs/STATUS.md` at start; update `STATUS.md`, commit, `git push` at end.\n# Workflow Expectations\n\n- When asked to **create, merge, or consolidate an artifact** (calendar, deck, doc), the expected deliverable is always a produced file. Verify what exists first, then create the artifact regardless — never conclude with \"it already exists\" instead of delivering the file.\n- When the user types `/graphify`, invoke the Skill tool with `skill: \"graphify\"` before doing anything else.\n\n# Session Startup\n\nHandled by the `keystone-unified-snapshot.js` SessionStart hook — do not read these files by hand. It injects `global-memory/USER.md`, `global-memory/MEMORY.md`, `transcripts/.errors.log` (only when non-empty), and a pointer to the KeystoneOS snapshot.\n\n- If `.errors.log` content appears, surface it once on the first response, then continue. It is normally empty; entries mean hook failures.\n- If a **\"SessionStart hook could not read a required memory file\"** block appears, say so on the first response instead of working around it.\n- **If no `## User profile` block is in context at session start, the hook did not fire.** Say so, then read `USER.md`, `MEMORY.md` and `transcripts/.errors.log` by hand.\n- What the hook injects is a frozen snapshot. Mid-session writes take effect next session. Never load more than this proactively.\n\n# Memory — routing only\n\nThe two-tier memory system (`~/.claude/global-memory/` and `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/`) is owned by the **`memory-write` skill**, which holds the tier rules, character caps, slug rules, write steps, retrieval ladder, and daily-compiled-log rules.\n\nThe active project's Tier A memory (`~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/`, indexed by its `MEMORY.md`) is **auto-injected by the harness** each session. Tier A files are also indexed by memsearch, so they stay recallable across projects.\n\n- Writing a fact — \"remember this\", \"note that\", \"save this\", \"forget about\" → `Skill(\"memory-write\")`\n- Recalling past context — \"did we already…?\", \"what did we decide about X\" → `Skill(\"memory-write\")`, retrieval-ladder section\n- Daily compiled logs at `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/daily/` are owned by `claude-memory-compiler`. Never hand-edit them; never read them eagerly.\n- **Slugs differ per machine** (Mac `-Users-kristinarash-DEV-<X>`, Windows `C--Users-eric-rash-Desktop-DEV-<X>`). Tier A written on one is not auto-injected on the other; memsearch still finds it. Durable project facts belong in the repo's `docs/` so both machines see them.\n\nDo not restate the memory procedure in this file. It has one home, and that home is the skill.\n\n---\n\n`AGENTS.md` is the sibling of this file for harnesses that have no Skill tool (Codex and similar). It intentionally carries the memory rules as plain prose instead of routing to a skill. **The two files are not meant to be identical — do not \"fix\" the difference by symlinking them.**"},{"path":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/machine.md","type":"User","content":"# Shell / Environment (macOS)\n\n- zsh/bash is the native surface on this machine. PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`, `/opt/homebrew/bin/pwsh`) is installed only to run the KeystoneOS `.ps1` jobs natively.\n- **The tool-path list has one home: `~/.claude/TOOLPATHS.md`.** Read that file when you need a path. Only the traps below are kept, because acting on them wrongly is expensive.\n- **Verify, don't trust any list.** `which -a <tool>` is authoritative. To read PATH honestly, scrub the environment first:\n  `env -i HOME=\"$HOME\" USER=\"$USER\" TERM=xterm SHELL=/bin/zsh /bin/zsh -lic 'echo $PATH'`\n- Traps:\n  - Python is PEP 668-managed. Use venvs or `uv`, never `pip install` globally.\n  - `uv` is a standalone install at `~/.local/bin/uv`, **not** Homebrew. Claude Code hooks call `$HOME/.local/bin/uv`; do not delete or relocate it.\n  - `timeout` is NOT installed (no GNU coreutils). Don't use it in scripts.\n  - Homebrew owns `/opt/homebrew/bin`. `/usr/local/bin` is empty and stays that way.\n  - Secrets live in the macOS Keychain, not in `~/.zshrc`.\n- DEV projects live at `~/DEV` (not `~/Desktop/DEV`). Workspace map: `~/DEV/CLAUDE.md`.\n- Primary storage drive (documents, data, deliverables): `~/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-eric.rash11@gmail.com/My Drive/00_Primary_Storage_Drive`."},{"path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/CLAUDE.md","type":"Project","content":"# ~/DEV — workspace map\n\nEric Rash — Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Sport science and\nfootball analytics, plus the agent system that runs them.\n\nThis file is a **map, not a rulebook**. Behavior rules live in\n`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`. Read only the index you need.\n\n## Areas\n\n| Area | Index | What lives there |\n|---|---|---|\n| Football | `FOOTBALL.md` | Game film, player analytics, CFB prediction. ~39k files — about two thirds of DEV. |\n| Performance | `PERFORMANCE.md` | Athlete monitoring, testing, load, risk. GPS, force plate, VALD, DEXA, S2. |\n| Research | `RESEARCH.md` | Literature, Zotero, book library, reading pipelines. |\n| Platform | `PLATFORM.md` | KeystoneOS, dashboards, CLIs, scheduled jobs. |\n| Program | `PROGRAM.md` | Staff reporting, curriculum, proposals, vetting, brand. |\n| Personal | `PERSONAL.md` | Side projects. Not Baylor work. |\n| Commands | `COMMANDS.md` | Desk reference: every report/project command Eric can run himself. |\n\n## Elsewhere on disk\n\n| Path | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/` | Parked projects. Not a git repo, so the consolidation sweep skips it. Has its own `README.md`. |\n| `~/DEV/_INBOX/` | Loose files from the `~/DEV` root. Own repo. |\n| `~/SecondBrain/` | Obsidian vault, ~850 notes. Own `CLAUDE.md`. |\n| `~/.claude/skills/` | ~155 skills |\n| `~/go/bin/` | 8 custom CLIs built with `cli-printing-press` |\n| `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/` | ~230 compiled articles |\n\n## Layout rules\n\nFull rules — grouping depth, the depth-2 discovery limit, and the gitignored-subtree\ntrap — live in project memory: `project_dev-layout-and-discovery.md`.\n\n- 23 projects carry their own `CLAUDE.md`. Read it before working in one.\n\n## Handling care\n\n- `DexaProject/` holds identifiable health data. It now lives on Google Drive at\n  `~/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-eric.rash11@gmail.com/My Drive/00_Primary_Storage_Drive/DexaProject`,\n  not in `~/DEV`. See `PERFORMANCE.md`.\n  Never paste its contents into chat, an artifact, or an external service.\n\n## Keeping this true\n\nWhen a file moves or a new project starts, **update the router in the same\nturn**. A stale pointer is worse than no pointer.\n\nThe counts above are approximate on purpose — they convey scale, not state, and\na `~` keeps ordinary growth from making this file wrong. Re-measure them only\nwhen one is off by enough to change a decision. Counts last checked 2026-09-05.\n\nMemory files named in these indexes (`project_*.md`, `user_*.md`) live at\n`~/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/`. They auto-load only when\ncwd is `~/DEV` itself — from inside a sub-project, open them by path.\n\nSearch everything with `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`."},{"path":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/MEMORY.md","type":"AutoMem","content":"# Memory Index\n\nOne line per memory: title, file, and a hook that says when to open it.\nNever put memory content here — the file itself holds the facts.\n\n- [Printing Press spec traps](project_printing-press-spec-traps.md) — writing a CLI spec: pagination, envelope fields, fixtures, doc regen\n- [zsh shell traps](project_zsh-shell-traps.md) — reserved vars, word-splitting, pipeline exit codes\n- [Status report sourcing](project_status-report-sourcing.md) — which source counts as \"Shipped\", which OKR doc is live\n- [Fireflies CLI on Mac](project_fireflies-cli-broken-on-mac.md) — \"out of memory (14)\", getting transcript text, renames that don't stick\n- [gws Google Forms API](project_gws-forms-api.md) — scopes, create vs batchUpdate, duplicated items\n- [Kenny Boyd doc review](project_kenny-boyd-doc-review-preferences.md) — tone and labelling rules for review docs\n- [Terminal Fluency workspace](project_terminal-fluency-workspace.md) — resume protocol and how he learns CLI\n- [PATH shadowing and cleanup](project_path-shadowing-and-cleanup.md) — measuring PATH honestly; before deleting any binary\n- [Backup/sync system](project_backup-sync-system.md) — manifest routing, verify-by-receipt, schedule history\n- [CFB Analytics Lab](project_cfb-analytics-lab.md) — resume protocol and CFBD API traps\n- [CFB forecast capture](project_cfb-forecast-capture.md) — Big 12 weekly freeze; one wrapper command; is a gap really a missed week?\n- [Drive FUSE rsync loop](project_drive-fuse-rsync-mtime-loop.md) — rsync re-sending identical files to Google Drive\n- [SecondBrain vault location](project_secondbrain-vault-location.md) — current path and what hardcodes it\n- [Milvus lock misdiagnosis](project_milvus-lock-misdiagnosis.md) — Zotero/Milvus errors; diagnose before rebuilding\n- [Memory compiler pipeline](project_memory-compiler-pipeline.md) — dead-flush tells, backfilling a date, \"Indexed 0 chunks\"\n- [memsearch index path drift](project_memsearch-index-path-drift.md) — silent fallback to fewer indexed paths\n- [memsearch hook timeout cap](project_memsearch-hook-timeout-cap.md) — session-end index timing out; a fixed cap outrun by corpus growth\n- [Morning briefing retired](project_morning-briefing-retired.md) — where the code, briefings and config went\n- [graphify runtime setup](project_graphify-runtime-setup.md) — venv location, install flags, rebuild triggers\n- [graphify semantic extraction](project_graphify-semantic-extraction.md) — quota traps and silent 429s\n- [Automation cadence](project_automation-cadence.md) — ritual times, reindex, audit due dates, gws/Gmail limits\n- [S2 Cognition analysis](project_s2-cognition-analysis.md) — norms scope, battery size, threshold discipline\n- [Sports teams](user_sports-teams.md) — default team for ambiguous WNBA asks\n- [Working-conditions bottleneck](user_working-conditions-bottleneck.md) — what limits him; the permission gate on acting unasked\n- [Zero-edit YouTube pipeline](reference_zero-edit-youtube-pipeline.md) — Jack Roberts' Claude Code video system\n- [DEV layout and discovery](project_dev-layout-and-discovery.md) — folder rules, depth-2 scan limit, gitignore trap\n- [KeystoneOS verification habits](project_keystoneos-verification-habits.md) — which state claims mislead; re-check before asserting; morning-cadence dies on a `C:\\` or URL `canonicalPath` in projects.json\n- [Portfolio registry provenance](project_portfolio-registry-write-provenance.md) — who writes portfolio.json; how to commit changes\n- [Command centre](project_command-centre.md) — localhost:7373 front door + the /os board, daemon health, backup default-deny\n- [Vendored skill install](project_vendored-skill-install.md) — installing a third-party skill for Claude Code + Codex + Cowork; the `-a` flag trap\n- [Vendored skill repair](project_vendored-skill-repair.md) — a skill returns empty output; which copy is actually live; patches that revert\n- [Personal site build routes](project_personal-site-build-routes.md) — Artifact constraints vs scroll-craft; where the standalone build lives\n- [Book scan page ordering](project_book-scan-page-ordering.md) — phone-scanned book pages out of order; folio OCR lies, read a contact sheet\n- [Ingest review gate](feedback_ingest-review-gate.md) — before ingest-book or any expensive atomization; what the arithmetic gate does and does not catch\n- [Dormant threads](project_dormant-threads.md) — Tezino CampPrep and FaceComps sim: where each stopped and what is still open\n- [RTP CLA course](project_rtp-cla-course.md) — book ingest state, the +13 page offset, the one rebuild still outstanding\n- [ATH Tech framework](project_ath-tech-framework.md) — the shipped 08-14 doc set, the five unwritten gaps, the ITS blocker\n- [Supervisor log pipeline](project_supervisor-log-pipeline.md) — nightly log feeding the Kenny/Chris report; action-items-are-not-outcomes trap\n- [dcg guard blocks](project_dcg-guard-blocks.md) — a Bash command was rejected outright; which shapes trip it and the rewrite that works\n- [Headless tool-deny gap](project_headless-claude-tool-deny-gap.md) — `claude -p --disallowedTools Bash` still runs shell via context-mode MCP; before calling a headless run read-only\n- [Config encoding damage from Windows](project_settings-json-bom-trap.md) — dcg cries \"hook missing\", or the statusline bars render as `â–“` junk; one bad write causes both, in settings.json AND CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md\n- [Tech inventory refresh](project_tech-inventory-refresh.md) — which forms platform is live, why gws cannot pull responses, the stale OneDrive copy, reminder sent 9/8, the refresh command\n- [Brain audit loop](project_brain-audit-loop.md) — scoring the second brain; why manual rituals die and cron ones live\n- [Hook two-copies trap](project_hook-two-copies-trap.md) — a hook patch \"reverts\"; `.claude/hooks/` and `.codex/hooks/` both need it\n- [Child dental plan](user_child-dental-plan.md) — 4yo cavities: pediatric dentist not yet booked, SDF vs n-HAp\n- [ELI5 output style](user_eli5-output-style.md) — concise plain-language output rules shared by Claude Code and Codex\n- [GitHub consolidation](project_github-consolidation.md) — ~/DEV on GitHub: the thin root repo, _ARCHIVE, where DexaProject went, merge traps\n- [Skill bloat and promotion](project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md) — before touching ANY skill folder: which of the three copies is canonical, why `~/.claude/skills` IS a git repo, the sync script that deletes, the 2026-09-12 audit, the 40 name-only skills (9/14), Pi/pi.dev facts\n- [Prioritization slate](project_prioritization-slate.md) — Eric proposes a new project or idea; the cap, charters, slate-check timer, clearing a nag with mark-done"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"0a038c16-d255-43fa-9781-6a2ece7a8d13","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:43:27.068Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCodebase and user instructions are shown below. Be sure to adhere to these instructions. IMPORTANT: These instructions OVERRIDE any default behavior and you MUST follow them exactly as written.\n\nContents of /Users/kristinarash/.claude/CLAUDE.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):\n\n# Machine\n\nMachine-specific shell, paths, and traps live in `~/.claude/machine.md` (gitignored; copy `machine.macos.md` or `machine.windows.md` to `machine.md` on a new machine). It is imported below.\n\n@machine.md\n\nTwo machines share this setup: the MacBook (`~/DEV`) and the Baylor Windows laptop (`~/Desktop/DEV`). Projects sync through GitHub (`erash11`), documents through Google Drive. Every project repo carries a Session routine in its `AGENTS.md`: `git pull` + read `docs/STATUS.md` at start; update `STATUS.md`, commit, `git push` at end.\n# Workflow Expectations\n\n- When asked to **create, merge, or consolidate an artifact** (calendar, deck, doc), the expected deliverable is always a produced file. Verify what exists first, then create the artifact regardless — never conclude with \"it already exists\" instead of delivering the file.\n- When the user types `/graphify`, invoke the Skill tool with `skill: \"graphify\"` before doing anything else.\n\n# Session Startup\n\nHandled by the `keystone-unified-snapshot.js` SessionStart hook — do not read these files by hand. It injects `global-memory/USER.md`, `global-memory/MEMORY.md`, `transcripts/.errors.log` (only when non-empty), and a pointer to the KeystoneOS snapshot.\n\n- If `.errors.log` content appears, surface it once on the first response, then continue. It is normally empty; entries mean hook failures.\n- If a **\"SessionStart hook could not read a required memory file\"** block appears, say so on the first response instead of working around it.\n- **If no `## User profile` block is in context at session start, the hook did not fire.** Say so, then read `USER.md`, `MEMORY.md` and `transcripts/.errors.log` by hand.\n- What the hook injects is a frozen snapshot. Mid-session writes take effect next session. Never load more than this proactively.\n\n# Memory — routing only\n\nThe two-tier memory system (`~/.claude/global-memory/` and `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/`) is owned by the **`memory-write` skill**, which holds the tier rules, character caps, slug rules, write steps, retrieval ladder, and daily-compiled-log rules.\n\nThe active project's Tier A memory (`~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/`, indexed by its `MEMORY.md`) is **auto-injected by the harness** each session. Tier A files are also indexed by memsearch, so they stay recallable across projects.\n\n- Writing a fact — \"remember this\", \"note that\", \"save this\", \"forget about\" → `Skill(\"memory-write\")`\n- Recalling past context — \"did we already…?\", \"what did we decide about X\" → `Skill(\"memory-write\")`, retrieval-ladder section\n- Daily compiled logs at `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/daily/` are owned by `claude-memory-compiler`. Never hand-edit them; never read them eagerly.\n- **Slugs differ per machine** (Mac `-Users-kristinarash-DEV-<X>`, Windows `C--Users-eric-rash-Desktop-DEV-<X>`). Tier A written on one is not auto-injected on the other; memsearch still finds it. Durable project facts belong in the repo's `docs/` so both machines see them.\n\nDo not restate the memory procedure in this file. It has one home, and that home is the skill.\n\n---\n\n`AGENTS.md` is the sibling of this file for harnesses that have no Skill tool (Codex and similar). It intentionally carries the memory rules as plain prose instead of routing to a skill. **The two files are not meant to be identical — do not \"fix\" the difference by symlinking them.**\n\nContents of /Users/kristinarash/.claude/machine.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):\n\n# Shell / Environment (macOS)\n\n- zsh/bash is the native surface on this machine. PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`, `/opt/homebrew/bin/pwsh`) is installed only to run the KeystoneOS `.ps1` jobs natively.\n- **The tool-path list has one home: `~/.claude/TOOLPATHS.md`.** Read that file when you need a path. Only the traps below are kept, because acting on them wrongly is expensive.\n- **Verify, don't trust any list.** `which -a <tool>` is authoritative. To read PATH honestly, scrub the environment first:\n  `env -i HOME=\"$HOME\" USER=\"$USER\" TERM=xterm SHELL=/bin/zsh /bin/zsh -lic 'echo $PATH'`\n- Traps:\n  - Python is PEP 668-managed. Use venvs or `uv`, never `pip install` globally.\n  - `uv` is a standalone install at `~/.local/bin/uv`, **not** Homebrew. Claude Code hooks call `$HOME/.local/bin/uv`; do not delete or relocate it.\n  - `timeout` is NOT installed (no GNU coreutils). Don't use it in scripts.\n  - Homebrew owns `/opt/homebrew/bin`. `/usr/local/bin` is empty and stays that way.\n  - Secrets live in the macOS Keychain, not in `~/.zshrc`.\n- DEV projects live at `~/DEV` (not `~/Desktop/DEV`). Workspace map: `~/DEV/CLAUDE.md`.\n- Primary storage drive (documents, data, deliverables): `~/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-eric.rash11@gmail.com/My Drive/00_Primary_Storage_Drive`.\n\nContents of /Users/kristinarash/DEV/CLAUDE.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# ~/DEV — workspace map\n\nEric Rash — Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Sport science and\nfootball analytics, plus the agent system that runs them.\n\nThis file is a **map, not a rulebook**. Behavior rules live in\n`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`. Read only the index you need.\n\n## Areas\n\n| Area | Index | What lives there |\n|---|---|---|\n| Football | `FOOTBALL.md` | Game film, player analytics, CFB prediction. ~39k files — about two thirds of DEV. |\n| Performance | `PERFORMANCE.md` | Athlete monitoring, testing, load, risk. GPS, force plate, VALD, DEXA, S2. |\n| Research | `RESEARCH.md` | Literature, Zotero, book library, reading pipelines. |\n| Platform | `PLATFORM.md` | KeystoneOS, dashboards, CLIs, scheduled jobs. |\n| Program | `PROGRAM.md` | Staff reporting, curriculum, proposals, vetting, brand. |\n| Personal | `PERSONAL.md` | Side projects. Not Baylor work. |\n| Commands | `COMMANDS.md` | Desk reference: every report/project command Eric can run himself. |\n\n## Elsewhere on disk\n\n| Path | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/` | Parked projects. Not a git repo, so the consolidation sweep skips it. Has its own `README.md`. |\n| `~/DEV/_INBOX/` | Loose files from the `~/DEV` root. Own repo. |\n| `~/SecondBrain/` | Obsidian vault, ~850 notes. Own `CLAUDE.md`. |\n| `~/.claude/skills/` | ~155 skills |\n| `~/go/bin/` | 8 custom CLIs built with `cli-printing-press` |\n| `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/` | ~230 compiled articles |\n\n## Layout rules\n\nFull rules — grouping depth, the depth-2 discovery limit, and the gitignored-subtree\ntrap — live in project memory: `project_dev-layout-and-discovery.md`.\n\n- 23 projects carry their own `CLAUDE.md`. Read it before working in one.\n\n## Handling care\n\n- `DexaProject/` holds identifiable health data. It now lives on Google Drive at\n  `~/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-eric.rash11@gmail.com/My Drive/00_Primary_Storage_Drive/DexaProject`,\n  not in `~/DEV`. See `PERFORMANCE.md`.\n  Never paste its contents into chat, an artifact, or an external service.\n\n## Keeping this true\n\nWhen a file moves or a new project starts, **update the router in the same\nturn**. A stale pointer is worse than no pointer.\n\nThe counts above are approximate on purpose — they convey scale, not state, and\na `~` keeps ordinary growth from making this file wrong. Re-measure them only\nwhen one is off by enough to change a decision. Counts last checked 2026-09-05.\n\nMemory files named in these indexes (`project_*.md`, `user_*.md`) live at\n`~/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/`. They auto-load only when\ncwd is `~/DEV` itself — from inside a sub-project, open them by path.\n\nSearch everything with `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`.\n\nContents of /Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/MEMORY.md (user's auto-memory, persists across conversations):\n\n# Memory Index\n\nOne line per memory: title, file, and a hook that says when to open it.\nNever put memory content here — the file itself holds the facts.\n\n- [Printing Press spec traps](project_printing-press-spec-traps.md) — writing a CLI spec: pagination, envelope fields, fixtures, doc regen\n- [zsh shell traps](project_zsh-shell-traps.md) — reserved vars, word-splitting, pipeline exit codes\n- [Status report sourcing](project_status-report-sourcing.md) — which source counts as \"Shipped\", which OKR doc is live\n- [Fireflies CLI on Mac](project_fireflies-cli-broken-on-mac.md) — \"out of memory (14)\", getting transcript text, renames that don't stick\n- [gws Google Forms API](project_gws-forms-api.md) — scopes, create vs batchUpdate, duplicated items\n- [Kenny Boyd doc review](project_kenny-boyd-doc-review-preferences.md) — tone and labelling rules for review docs\n- [Terminal Fluency workspace](project_terminal-fluency-workspace.md) — resume protocol and how he learns CLI\n- [PATH shadowing and cleanup](project_path-shadowing-and-cleanup.md) — measuring PATH honestly; before deleting any binary\n- [Backup/sync system](project_backup-sync-system.md) — manifest routing, verify-by-receipt, schedule history\n- [CFB Analytics Lab](project_cfb-analytics-lab.md) — resume protocol and CFBD API traps\n- [CFB forecast capture](project_cfb-forecast-capture.md) — Big 12 weekly freeze; one wrapper command; is a gap really a missed week?\n- [Drive FUSE rsync loop](project_drive-fuse-rsync-mtime-loop.md) — rsync re-sending identical files to Google Drive\n- [SecondBrain vault location](project_secondbrain-vault-location.md) — current path and what hardcodes it\n- [Milvus lock misdiagnosis](project_milvus-lock-misdiagnosis.md) — Zotero/Milvus errors; diagnose before rebuilding\n- [Memory compiler pipeline](project_memory-compiler-pipeline.md) — dead-flush tells, backfilling a date, \"Indexed 0 chunks\"\n- [memsearch index path drift](project_memsearch-index-path-drift.md) — silent fallback to fewer indexed paths\n- [memsearch hook timeout cap](project_memsearch-hook-timeout-cap.md) — session-end index timing out; a fixed cap outrun by corpus growth\n- [Morning briefing retired](project_morning-briefing-retired.md) — where the code, briefings and config went\n- [graphify runtime setup](project_graphify-runtime-setup.md) — venv location, install flags, rebuild triggers\n- [graphify semantic extraction](project_graphify-semantic-extraction.md) — quota traps and silent 429s\n- [Automation cadence](project_automation-cadence.md) — ritual times, reindex, audit due dates, gws/Gmail limits\n- [S2 Cognition analysis](project_s2-cognition-analysis.md) — norms scope, battery size, threshold discipline\n- [Sports teams](user_sports-teams.md) — default team for ambiguous WNBA asks\n- [Working-conditions bottleneck](user_working-conditions-bottleneck.md) — what limits him; the permission gate on acting unasked\n- [Zero-edit YouTube pipeline](reference_zero-edit-youtube-pipeline.md) — Jack Roberts' Claude Code video system\n- [DEV layout and discovery](project_dev-layout-and-discovery.md) — folder rules, depth-2 scan limit, gitignore trap\n- [KeystoneOS verification habits](project_keystoneos-verification-habits.md) — which state claims mislead; re-check before asserting; morning-cadence dies on a `C:\\` or URL `canonicalPath` in projects.json\n- [Portfolio registry provenance](project_portfolio-registry-write-provenance.md) — who writes portfolio.json; how to commit changes\n- [Command centre](project_command-centre.md) — localhost:7373 front door + the /os board, daemon health, backup default-deny\n- [Vendored skill install](project_vendored-skill-install.md) — installing a third-party skill for Claude Code + Codex + Cowork; the `-a` flag trap\n- [Vendored skill repair](project_vendored-skill-repair.md) — a skill returns empty output; which copy is actually live; patches that revert\n- [Personal site build routes](project_personal-site-build-routes.md) — Artifact constraints vs scroll-craft; where the standalone build lives\n- [Book scan page ordering](project_book-scan-page-ordering.md) — phone-scanned book pages out of order; folio OCR lies, read a contact sheet\n- [Ingest review gate](feedback_ingest-review-gate.md) — before ingest-book or any expensive atomization; what the arithmetic gate does and does not catch\n- [Dormant threads](project_dormant-threads.md) — Tezino CampPrep and FaceComps sim: where each stopped and what is still open\n- [RTP CLA course](project_rtp-cla-course.md) — book ingest state, the +13 page offset, the one rebuild still outstanding\n- [ATH Tech framework](project_ath-tech-framework.md) — the shipped 08-14 doc set, the five unwritten gaps, the ITS blocker\n- [Supervisor log pipeline](project_supervisor-log-pipeline.md) — nightly log feeding the Kenny/Chris report; action-items-are-not-outcomes trap\n- [dcg guard blocks](project_dcg-guard-blocks.md) — a Bash command was rejected outright; which shapes trip it and the rewrite that works\n- [Headless tool-deny gap](project_headless-claude-tool-deny-gap.md) — `claude -p --disallowedTools Bash` still runs shell via context-mode MCP; before calling a headless run read-only\n- [Config encoding damage from Windows](project_settings-json-bom-trap.md) — dcg cries \"hook missing\", or the statusline bars render as `â–“` junk; one bad write causes both, in settings.json AND CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md\n- [Tech inventory refresh](project_tech-inventory-refresh.md) — which forms platform is live, why gws cannot pull responses, the stale OneDrive copy, reminder sent 9/8, the refresh command\n- [Brain audit loop](project_brain-audit-loop.md) — scoring the second brain; why manual rituals die and cron ones live\n- [Hook two-copies trap](project_hook-two-copies-trap.md) — a hook patch \"reverts\"; `.claude/hooks/` and `.codex/hooks/` both need it\n- [Child dental plan](user_child-dental-plan.md) — 4yo cavities: pediatric dentist not yet booked, SDF vs n-HAp\n- [ELI5 output style](user_eli5-output-style.md) — concise plain-language output rules shared by Claude Code and Codex\n- [GitHub consolidation](project_github-consolidation.md) — ~/DEV on GitHub: the thin root repo, _ARCHIVE, where DexaProject went, merge traps\n- [Skill bloat and promotion](project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md) — before touching ANY skill folder: which of the three copies is canonical, why `~/.claude/skills` IS a git repo, the sync script that deletes, the 2026-09-12 audit, the 40 name-only skills (9/14), Pi/pi.dev facts\n- [Prioritization slate](project_prioritization-slate.md) — Eric proposes a new project or idea; the cap, charters, slate-check timer, clearing a nag with mark-done\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"35d4f785-606b-402b-829e-4e9c3081934b","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"35d4f785-606b-402b-829e-4e9c3081934b","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
